onal activation can worsen renal sodium retention. Acute triggers may include ischaemia, arrhythmia, infection, uncontrolled blood pressure, medication interruption, or renal deterioration. This topic is educational and should be interpreted alongside current local pathways and patient-specific assessment.","keyMechanisms":["Raised filling pressures cause pulmonary or systemic venous congestion","impaired output and neurohormonal activation can worsen renal sodium retention. Acute triggers may include ischaemia, arrhythmia, infection, uncontrolled blood pressure, medication interruption, or renal deterioration."]},"presentation":{"symptoms":["Evaluate the characteristic symptom pattern of Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema, including onset, evolution, severity, associated symptoms, precipitating factors, complications, and relevant negative findings.","Clarify onset, progression, associated symptoms, baseline function, medicines and the patient’s own priorities.","Ask about relevant risk factors and context described in the case; document important negative findings rather than assuming their absence."],"physicalSigns":["Record observations and a focused examination directed to the organ system and competing diagnoses in this topic.","Assess functional change, comorbidity, hydration/perfusion where relevant, and baseline status.","Use serial reassessment when the condition or treatment can change over hours or days."],"redFlags":["Severe hypoxaemia or exhaustion","Hypotension, cool peripheries, or shock","Ongoing ischaemic chest pain or dangerous arrhythmia","Altered consciousness or rapidly worsening respiratory distress"]},"differentials":[{"condition":"Pneumonia","distinguishingFeatures":"Fever or focal findings may coexist with congestion."},{"condition":"Pulmonary embolism","distinguishingFeatures":"Acute pleuritic symptoms and thromboembolic risk require structured assessment."},{"condition":"COPD/asthma exacerbation","distinguishingFeatures":"Wheeze does not exclude simultaneous cardiac congestion."}],"investigations":{"ruleIn":[{"tier":"specializedAdjunctive","testName":"ECG, oxygenation, chest imaging, renal/electrolyte profile, natriuretic peptide and cardiac biomarkers as indicated.","modalityTarget":"ECG, oxygenation, chest imaging, renal/electrolyte profile, natriuretic peptide and cardiac biomarkers as indicated. — Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","targetCondition":"Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","expectedFinding":"A result that supports the working diagnosis or defines its severity, interpreted with the clinical picture and current criteria.","clinicalRationale":"Choose this investigation because it can confirm the suspected process, establish baseline risk, or change the next decision in this case."},{"tier":"specializedAdjunctive","testName":"Bedside cardiac or lung ultrasound can support assessment where trained staff and local pathways are available.","modalityTarget":"Bedside cardiac or lung ultrasound can support assessment where trained staff and local pathways are available. — Cause, complication, or risk relevant to Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","targetCondition":"Cause, complication, or risk relevant to Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","expectedFinding":"A result that may identify a precipitating factor or complication; interpret in context rather than as a stand-alone diagnosis.","clinicalRationale":"This helps detect an important alternative or complication and informs safe monitoring and referral."}],"ruleOut":[{"tier":"laboratory","testName":"Focused evaluation for the leading alternative diagnoses","modalityTarget":"Focused evaluation for the leading alternative diagnoses — Important alternative to Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","targetCondition":"Important alternative to Acute Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","expectedFinding":"Findings that lower the likelihood of the selected alternative; a normal result does not exclude every urgent 